-- work_career (17)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A junior analyst discovers her manager is taking credit for her work in board meetings. She schedules a meeting with the manager’s boss, brings receipts, and the manager is removed from the project. The team is reorganized around her.', e'Was she justified going over her manager?', 'Work · Hierarchy', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Hard work credited to someone else is theft, quietly.', 'There were steps that should have come first.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'An engineer refuses to ship a product on the date marketing has promised customers, because she believes it will fail in the field. The launch is delayed by three weeks. Revenue takes a quarter to recover. The product, however, works.', e'Was she right to hold the line?', 'Work · Quality', 'Right to hold', 'Wrong to hold', 'Engineers exist precisely to say no when no is the right answer.', 'A company that ships nothing on time eventually ships nothing.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A founder fires an early employee who has been at the company for six years. The employee’s skills no longer match the role. The founder offers an above-market severance and an honest reference. The employee says he was promised a home.', e'Was the firing justified?', 'Startup · Loyalty', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A small company cannot afford emotional payroll.', 'Six years of risk should buy a better landing than a check.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A manager learns that a team member is interviewing elsewhere. He confronts her, then quietly removes her from a key project before she has resigned. She finds out and resigns the next day.', e'Was the manager right to act early?', 'Work · Trust', 'Right to act', 'Wrong to act', 'A team can plan only around people who are still on it.', 'You do not pre-fire someone for thinking about leaving.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A senior designer takes a six-month unpaid sabbatical to care for her mother. She returns to find she has been quietly demoted — same title, smaller scope. HR calls it a re-leveling. She files a complaint.', e'Was the company justified?', 'Work · Caregiving', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Roles drift while people are away — that is structure, not punishment.', 'A sabbatical is not a resignation; the company knew it.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A salesperson closes a major deal by quietly accommodating a request from the buyer that violates company policy on discounts. His boss approves the deal anyway. A junior colleague reports the violation. The salesperson is fired; the boss is not.', e'Was the firing fair?', 'Work · Rules', 'Fair', 'Unfair', 'Rules exist so that even successful violations have a cost.', 'A junior fired while a senior survives is a message about the company.', 'work_career', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A startup founder pays her co-founder less than herself for the first three years, citing his late join date. They split the company evenly when he equals her hours. He later asks for back-pay equalization. She refuses.', e'Was she right to refuse?', 'Startup · Equity', 'Right to refuse', 'Wrong to refuse', 'Risk taken earlier is risk worth more.', 'A partner pulled even should be made whole.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A junior employee turns down a promotion because the new role will require working under a specific senior who has a reputation for cruelty. The company says they cannot guarantee anything about future managers.', e'Was she right to refuse?', 'Work · Self-protection', 'Right to refuse', 'Wrong to refuse', 'A title is not worth a year of psychological damage.', 'Careers are built on uncomfortable bets, not careful refusals.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A manager assigns the team’s least-glamorous work consistently to one person, who happens to be the most competent at it. After two years, that person is passed over for promotion because his work was not visible.', e'Was the manager wrong?', 'Work · Recognition', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'A good manager protects competent people from invisibility.', 'Promotions follow visibility; that is how organizations work.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A new hire reports a senior colleague’s casual racist remark to HR on her first week. HR opens an investigation. The senior colleague is suspended. Several colleagues stop speaking to the new hire.', e'Was the new hire right to escalate?', 'Work · Speech', 'Right to escalate', 'Wrong to escalate', 'A remark unchallenged is a remark normalized.', 'A first-week complaint costs years of trust.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A consultant doubles her rate after one viral case study. Half her existing clients cannot afford her any more. She loses friendships built over the engagement years. Her new clients pay without flinching.', e'Was the price hike justified?', 'Work · Worth', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Pricing follows demand, not friendship.', 'A consultant who sheds the people who built her is not a consultant.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A senior engineer privately tells a junior her code is the worst he has reviewed in years. He thinks he is being direct and helpful. She files a complaint about a hostile environment.', e'Was the complaint warranted?', 'Work · Feedback', 'Warranted', 'Not warranted', 'Cruel feedback is feedback that fails to land.', 'Honest review is the floor of a real craft.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A bootstrapped founder takes a 90% pay cut for two years to keep her three employees fully paid through a downturn. She survives. When the company recovers, none of the three offer to defer raises so she can recoup.', e'Was she right to expect reciprocity?', 'Startup · Loyalty', 'Right to expect', 'Wrong to expect', 'A founder who carries a team should not be the only one who pays.', 'Employees did not sign up for ownership; she did.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A reporter quits a paper after her editor refuses to run her year-long investigation. She publishes the piece on a free platform. The paper sues her for the work product.', e'Was the reporter justified?', 'Work · Ownership', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Journalism unpublished is not journalism.', 'Work done for a paper is the paper’s, not yours to take.', 'work_career', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A manager has an unwritten policy of never hiring anyone who took more than a year off from work. A candidate confronts her about it after being rejected. The manager refuses to comment.', e'Was the policy defensible?', 'Hiring · Bias', 'Defensible', 'Indefensible', 'Continuity is a legitimate signal for some roles.', 'A blanket no on careers paused is a quiet bias dressed up as judgment.', 'work_career', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A startup CEO publicly fires an executive on a livestreamed all-hands so the team sees the line that was crossed. The executive sues. The team is split: some say it was clarifying, some say it was humiliation.', e'Was the public firing justified?', 'Work · Authority', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A norm is only real if breaking it is visible.', 'A person fired in public is a person broken for a lesson.', 'work_career', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A team lead refuses to take any meetings before 10 AM, citing his mental health. The team has a major customer in a time zone that requires early calls. He says the customer can wait.', e'Was he right to hold the line?', 'Work · Wellbeing', 'Right to hold', 'Wrong to hold', 'A worker without limits is a worker who burns down soon.', 'A lead is paid to take the calls others cannot.', 'work_career', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

