-- society_politics (17)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A small town puts up a memorial to a soldier who is also remembered locally for years of domestic abuse. The town debates whether to take it down. The mayor leaves it up but adds a plaque acknowledging both lives.', e'Was the mayor’s solution right?', 'Memory · Civic', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A memorial that holds both truths is more honest than either alone.', 'Public honor for an abuser, with footnotes, is still honor.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A city council bans loud political rallies after 9 PM, including those by ruling-party allies. Smaller parties argue the rule disproportionately hurts them, since their supporters work until late.', e'Was the rule justified?', 'Civic · Speech', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A quiet city at night is a basic civic floor.', 'Neutral rules can still tilt against people without daytime hours.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge rules that a candidate convicted of a non-violent offense twenty years ago can run for mayor. Opponents call it a loophole. Supporters call it a long-overdue right.', e'Was the ruling correct?', 'Politics · Redemption', 'Correct', 'Incorrect', 'A debt paid is a debt paid; democracy is not a permanent ledger.', 'Public office is a privilege that history can disqualify.', 'society_politics', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A mayor declares a public holiday to honor a local artist who refused a national award twenty years ago. Critics call it political; supporters call it overdue.', e'Was the holiday justified?', 'Civic · Honor', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A town can choose what it honors and when.', 'Public holidays for living people are political weapons.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A protest movement shuts down a metro line for two days. Commuters lose wages. Hospitals reroute ambulances. The movement insists the disruption is the point.', e'Was the disruption justified?', 'Civic · Dissent', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A protest you can ignore is a protest that has already failed.', 'A movement that hurts the people it claims to defend is a vanity.', 'society_politics', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A national government bans a foreign newspaper from operating, citing repeated false reporting. Free-press groups condemn the move. Government supporters call it overdue accountability.', e'Was the ban justified?', 'Press · State', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A press without facts is not a press; it is a weapon.', 'Bans are the move of a government that has lost the argument.', 'society_politics', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A small village passes a rule fining anyone who plays loud music after 8 PM. A community known for late-evening gatherings is disproportionately fined. The rule is neutral on paper.', e'Was the rule fair?', 'Civic · Neutrality', 'Fair', 'Unfair', 'A rule that applies to everyone is the definition of fairness.', 'Fairness on paper is not fairness in effect.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A city refuses to enforce a national rule it considers unjust. The state government threatens to withhold funds. The city stands firm.', e'Was the city right?', 'Civic · Federalism', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A city closest to a rule’s harm has a duty to resist.', 'A federation works only when its parts do not pick rules à la carte.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A university quietly removes a renowned professor’s endowed chair after old writings of his resurface. He claims the writings are decades old and badly misread. The university issues no public statement.', e'Was the removal justified?', 'Speech · Consequence', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A chair is an honor; honors can be revisited.', 'Punishment without explanation is closer to cowardice than to justice.', 'society_politics', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A nonprofit refuses a large donation from a corporation whose practices contradict the nonprofit’s mission. The nonprofit will have to lay off staff. The board votes for principle over payroll.', e'Was the board right?', 'Civic · Money', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A nonprofit funded by its enemy is no longer the same nonprofit.', 'Principles you cannot afford are not principles, they are vanity.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A government uses face-recognition data to identify and arrest protest organizers after a large rally. The arrests are legal under existing law. Civil-society groups demand the data be deleted.', e'Was the use of the data justified?', 'State · Surveillance', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A rally that breaks the law has no claim to anonymity.', 'A democracy that recognizes its protesters is not a democracy for long.', 'society_politics', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A new neighborhood association votes to ban any election posters within the community, citing aesthetics. A few residents say their right to speech is being curtailed by their own neighbors.', e'Was the ban defensible?', 'Civic · Speech', 'Defensible', 'Indefensible', 'A community has a right to set its visual rules.', 'A speech rule passed by neighbors is still a speech rule.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A state legislator votes against his party on a major bill that affects his constituents. He loses the party whip. His constituents re-elect him with a larger margin the following year.', e'Was the vote justified?', 'Politics · Loyalty', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A representative who cannot break ranks is a postal worker for the party.', 'A party that cannot whip its own is a party that cannot govern.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A national leader pardons a former colleague convicted of corruption, citing personal trust. The colleague did not request the pardon. The opposition calls it the final blow to the country’s anti-corruption framework.', e'Was the pardon justified?', 'Politics · Power', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Pardon is an executive power; it exists precisely for cases like this.', 'A pardon for a friend is the moment the public stops trusting the office.', 'society_politics', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A district court refuses to enforce a high court order, citing implementation difficulties. The high court calls it contempt. The district judge says she will accept any punishment.', e'Was she right to refuse?', 'Law · Hierarchy', 'Right to refuse', 'Wrong to refuse', 'Conscience above hierarchy is the heart of a real judiciary.', 'A judge who chooses which orders to follow is no longer a judge.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A mayor uses public funds to build a small park in a constituency that did not vote for her. Opponents say it is bribery; supporters say it is the job.', e'Was the spending justified?', 'Civic · Governance', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A mayor governs all of her city, including those who opposed her.', 'Public spending allocated for political gain is political even when it builds parks.', 'society_politics', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A national broadcaster refuses to cover a regional flood because the affected region is hostile to the ruling party. A small independent channel covers it instead and is shut down a month later for an unrelated licensing issue.', e'Was the broadcaster wrong?', 'Press · Power', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'A public broadcaster that covers only its friends is propaganda.', 'Editorial independence sometimes means uneven coverage; that is not a crime.', 'society_politics', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

