-- justice_law (17)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A judge gives a first-time offender a suspended sentence after he steals food for his family during a regional drought. The prosecutor appeals, citing equal treatment.', e'Was the sentence right?', 'Law · Mercy', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A court that cannot see context is a court that cannot see.', 'Equal treatment is the floor of a justice system, not the ceiling.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A lawyer takes on the defense of a man widely believed to have committed a horrific crime, despite social pressure to refuse. She wins an acquittal on procedural grounds. She is shunned in her community.', e'Was she right to defend him?', 'Law · Duty', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A defense for the worst client is the test of the system, not its scandal.', 'A lawyer is not obliged to take every brief.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A police officer ignores a minor offense — a teenager smoking in a park — and gives a warning instead. A supervisor later disciplines the officer for inconsistent enforcement. The teenager goes home unharmed.', e'Was the supervisor right?', 'Policing · Discretion', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Discretion applied unevenly is bias dressed up as kindness.', 'A police force without judgment is not a police force.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A jury finds a defendant not guilty despite overwhelming evidence, because the jury believes the law itself is unjust. The verdict stands. Legal scholars debate it for a decade.', e'Was the jury right?', 'Law · Conscience', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Jury conscience is the last check on bad laws.', 'A jury that decides which laws to enforce is not a jury; it is a legislature.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A prosecutor offers a plea deal that reduces a violent offender’s sentence by ten years in exchange for testimony against a co-conspirator. The victim’s family is not consulted. The deal closes the case.', e'Was the deal justified?', 'Law · Bargain', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A long sentence on the bigger fish is worth the trade.', 'Justice you can negotiate down is not justice.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge imposes the maximum penalty on a young offender to “send a message” to a wave of similar crimes. The offender appeals on the grounds that he is being punished for others’ acts.', e'Was the sentence justified?', 'Law · Deterrence', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A sentence that prevents future harm is a sentence doing its job.', 'A person is not a billboard for someone else’s message.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge orders the deletion of a wrongfully convicted man’s record after thirty years. The decision is unanimous. The man asks for the court order to be framed and read aloud in court.', e'Was reading it aloud appropriate?', 'Law · Memory', 'Appropriate', 'Inappropriate', 'A wrongful conviction undone is a ritual the public should hear.', 'A courtroom is not a stage; quiet correction is its own dignity.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A small town’s police chief refuses to enforce a state law he believes violates basic rights. He is suspended. Half the force resigns in solidarity.', e'Was he right to refuse?', 'Law · Conscience', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A police chief is a citizen first, an officer second.', 'A chief who picks which laws to enforce is not a chief.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A child witnesses a violent crime. Defense lawyers argue against putting her on the stand, citing trauma; the prosecution argues her testimony is decisive. The judge calls a closed hearing.', e'Should she have been called?', 'Law · Witnesses', 'Called', 'Not called', 'Justice for the victim sometimes requires the witnesses available.', 'A child traumatized for a verdict has been traumatized twice.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A high court rules that a celebrity cannot be tried in absentia for ongoing financial crimes. The celebrity is abroad and unlikely to return. Victims of the alleged fraud number in the thousands.', e'Was the ruling correct?', 'Law · Absence', 'Correct', 'Incorrect', 'Trial in absentia is a hollow trial; the principle holds.', 'A man rich enough to leave can buy his own immunity.', 'justice_law', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A national database of accused — not convicted — sexual offenders is created. Civil-liberties groups call it a list of the innocent until proven otherwise. Victim advocates call it overdue.', e'Was the database justified?', 'Law · Reputation', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Patterns of accusation are themselves data.', 'A list of the not-yet-convicted is a list of the not-yet-vindicated.', 'justice_law', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A man is wrongly imprisoned for eleven years. On release, he sues for compensation. The state offers a one-time payment that does not match his lost earnings. He refuses and continues to sue.', e'Was he right to refuse?', 'Law · Reparation', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A state that took eleven years should give them back, in full.', 'A bird in hand is the only currency state suits ever pay.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge in a minor traffic case routinely waives fines for first-time offenders who can prove low income. A higher court rules the practice creates two classes of justice.', e'Was the higher court right?', 'Law · Equality', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A fine that crushes one citizen and tickles another is two fines.', 'A judge who decides by income is a judge running their own welfare system.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A public defender drops a client mid-trial after discovering the client lied about the central facts. The judge allows the withdrawal. The client is convicted within a week.', e'Was the withdrawal justified?', 'Law · Trust', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A lawyer cannot present what she now knows is a lie.', 'Even liars get a defense; the system runs on that.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A small-claims judge orders a landlord to pay her tenant interest on a delayed security deposit refund. The amount is minor. The judge calls it a precedent for the city.', e'Was the order justified?', 'Law · Tenant', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A small order is how a city signals what it cares about.', 'Courts should not micro-manage rental markets one tenant at a time.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge refuses to allow a victim’s family to read an impact statement in court, calling it inflammatory. The family says they have been silenced. The defendant is sentenced anyway.', e'Was the refusal right?', 'Law · Voice', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A courtroom is not therapy; the verdict is the verdict.', 'A victim’s voice is the one the system most often loses.', 'justice_law', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A regional court sets a precedent that whistleblowers cannot be fired for surfacing wrongdoing, even if their disclosures violated a confidentiality clause. The state appeals.', e'Was the precedent right?', 'Law · Truth', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A society protects whistleblowers because it cannot protect itself otherwise.', 'A contract signed is a contract that should hold.', 'justice_law', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- health_medicine (17)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A surgeon refuses to operate on a patient who twenty years earlier had been jailed for assaulting her brother. Another surgeon takes the case the same day. The hospital opens an inquiry.', e'Was she right to refuse?', 'Medicine · Conscience', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A surgeon is a person too; her oath does not erase what she has lived.', 'Her oath came before her past.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A doctor in a rural clinic lies to a patient about a terminal diagnosis at the family’s request. The patient lives six months longer than expected. He dies believing he was recovering.', e'Was the doctor right?', 'Medicine · Truth', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Some peace is worth more than perfect information.', 'A patient deserves to know what his last months are for.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A nurse refuses to administer a treatment she believes will harm a particular patient, despite the doctor’s order. She is suspended pending review. The patient is treated by another nurse.', e'Was she right to refuse?', 'Medicine · Hierarchy', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A nurse closest to a patient is sometimes the only check on a doctor.', 'A hospital that lets nurses veto doctors is a hospital that cannot function.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A hospital introduces a triage protocol during a critical bed shortage that prioritizes patients with longer expected lifespans. Older patients receive care later. Mortality rates among the elderly rise.', e'Was the protocol justified?', 'Medicine · Triage', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A scarce bed serving fifty more years is a different equation.', 'A hospital that ranks lives by years left is a hospital that has stopped being one.', 'health_medicine', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A doctor tells a pregnant patient her child will be born with a serious condition. The mother chooses to continue the pregnancy. The doctor tells colleagues she has “delayed an avoidable tragedy.” It is reported.', e'Was the comment a breach?', 'Medicine · Speech', 'Breach', 'Not a breach', 'A doctor’s private contempt for a patient is itself unsafe care.', 'A private remark to colleagues is not a public statement.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A teen comes to a clinic alone, asking for contraception. The doctor provides it without informing the parents, citing the patient’s confidentiality. The parents discover the prescription weeks later and sue.', e'Was the doctor right?', 'Medicine · Consent', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A teenager asking is a teenager who needs the consultation.', 'A minor is a minor; the parents are the legal authority.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A research hospital quietly enrolls patients in a study using their de-identified records. No individual is identifiable. The hospital does not seek consent. A journalist exposes the practice.', e'Was the hospital wrong?', 'Medicine · Data', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'Data about you, even anonymized, is still about you.', 'Medical progress lives on records used quietly and well.', 'health_medicine', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A surgeon publicly criticizes a colleague who lost a patient on the table. The criticism is medically accurate. The colleague resigns within a week. The surgical department becomes harder to recruit for.', e'Was the criticism right?', 'Medicine · Accountability', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A surgeon who covers for a peer puts the next patient at risk.', 'A profession that eats its own loses the next decade of surgeons.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A medical board strips a doctor’s license after he prescribed unapproved treatments that worked for a small number of patients. Some of his patients organize a campaign to restore the license.', e'Was the board right?', 'Medicine · Approval', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Approval exists because anecdotes are not data.', 'A doctor who helps is a doctor who helps; the patients know.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A psychiatrist refuses to break confidentiality despite a patient confessing detailed violent fantasies about his ex-partner. The patient has no record of violence. The psychiatrist warns the patient and continues sessions.', e'Was the psychiatrist right?', 'Medicine · Risk', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Confidentiality is the door that lets dangerous men enter therapy.', 'A psychiatrist with that warning has a duty to act on it.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A hospital quietly transfers a patient with an aggressive infection to another facility, knowing the receiving hospital has poorer infection control. The transfer is legal. Two staff resign.', e'Was the transfer justified?', 'Medicine · Risk', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A hospital protecting itself protects all of its other patients.', 'A transfer that exports harm is not care.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A doctor faces a patient who repeatedly refuses life-saving treatment for religious reasons. The doctor honors the refusal. The patient dies. The family sues the doctor for not “trying harder.”', e'Was the doctor right?', 'Medicine · Autonomy', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A patient with capacity has the right to refuse, even unto death.', 'A doctor’s job is to fight for life when family cannot.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A public hospital limits the number of times a patient with a chronic condition can be admitted in a year, citing capacity. The patient finds a workaround through a private hospital. He cannot afford it long-term.', e'Was the limit justified?', 'Medicine · Capacity', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A public system has to be triaged or it serves nobody well.', 'A limit on the chronically ill is a limit on staying alive.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A pharmaceutical company prices a life-saving drug at a level no public system in a region can afford. The company offers a steep discount in exchange for a multi-decade exclusive contract. The government refuses.', e'Was the government right?', 'Medicine · Cost', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A long contract for cheap medicine is a long contract for one company.', 'A drug at any price is better than no drug at all.', 'health_medicine', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A young oncologist quietly tells a patient her own honest opinion that the recommended treatment will likely not work, contradicting the team consensus. The patient changes course. The patient lives longer.', e'Was the oncologist right?', 'Medicine · Voice', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A doctor who buries a doubt is not a doctor; she is a clerk.', 'A team consensus exists for a reason; a junior should not undermine it.', 'health_medicine', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A vaccine clinic offers small cash incentives to encourage uptake during an outbreak. Some bioethicists object. The clinic argues it works.', e'Was the incentive justified?', 'Medicine · Choice', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A nudge that ends an outbreak is a nudge worth the cash.', 'A medical decision shaped by money is no longer freely chosen.', 'health_medicine', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A medical school rejects a brilliant candidate after he privately writes online that he will never treat a particular community. The school says character is part of the criteria.', e'Was the rejection justified?', 'Medicine · Character', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A doctor is a doctor for everyone or he is none.', 'A school does not get to police every private statement of its applicants.', 'health_medicine', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- money_wealth (17)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A landlord raises rent by 30% in a fast-changing neighborhood, knowing most current tenants cannot afford it. The new tenants pay without complaint. The old ones disperse.', e'Was the raise justified?', 'Money · Property', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A landlord prices a property at its market; that is the market’s job.', 'A landlord who clears a building of its neighbors clears the neighborhood.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A man inherits a large sum from a distant aunt. He chooses not to tell his wife and quietly puts most of it into an account in his name. Three years later, the marriage ends.', e'Was hiding it justified?', 'Money · Marriage', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'An inheritance is personal money, by every standard except resentment.', 'A shared life requires shared books.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A woman in financial trouble lies to her parents about why she needs a loan, saying it is for a business when it is to cover personal debt. They lend without questions. She pays them back within a year.', e'Was the lie justified?', 'Money · Family', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Outcomes paid back in full are their own kind of honesty.', 'A lie to family is a lie that compounds even when the money does.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A friend group plans an expensive trip. One member is between jobs and cannot afford it. The others offer to cover his share. He declines and stays home. The trip is awkward.', e'Was he right to decline?', 'Money · Pride', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A friendship that needs to be subsidized is no longer a friendship in equilibrium.', 'A holiday paid for in friendship is still a holiday earned.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A start-up founder takes a series-A round at a valuation she privately believes is unsustainable. She uses the cash to pay her team for three years, then declares bankruptcy. Investors lose their stake.', e'Was she right to take the deal?', 'Money · Risk', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Three years of payroll for fifty people is a real outcome.', 'A founder who took money on bad faith is a founder who lied.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A wealthy man donates a large sum to a public hospital on the condition that a wing is named after his late wife. The hospital accepts. A few staff resign in protest of the conditions.', e'Was the deal justified?', 'Money · Giving', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A donation that names a wing is still a donation that built a wing.', 'Public goods purchased with a name attached are no longer public.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A delivery worker discovers an envelope with a large amount of cash in the package he is delivering. He returns it intact. The sender, a wealthy investor, tips him a small fraction of what was inside.', e'Was the tip insulting?', 'Money · Decency', 'Insulting', 'Not insulting', 'A return of integrity deserves more than rounding error.', 'A tip is a tip; honesty is not a market item.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A man hides a redundancy payout from his family for six months while he looks for new work. He covers daily expenses from the payout so no one notices. His teenage children later learn he lied.', e'Was he right to hide it?', 'Money · Shame', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A family in panic helps nothing; a quiet runway helps everything.', 'A father who hides a layoff teaches his children that money is shameful.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A small business pays one employee 40% more than the others for the same role because she negotiated harder. A junior employee discovers this. The owner refuses to adjust.', e'Was the owner right?', 'Money · Pay', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Pay rewards what you ask for, not what you do not.', 'A company that pays differently for the same work has lied about the job.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A pension fund manager rebalances out of a major bank weeks before the bank fails. He had no insider information; he simply had a strong feeling. Regulators investigate his trades.', e'Was the rebalance defensible?', 'Money · Markets', 'Defensible', 'Indefensible', 'A manager is paid to have judgments and act on them.', 'Lucky timing is the cousin of a crime; investigators are right to ask.', 'money_wealth', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A pension fund manager invests aggressively to recover losses during a downturn. The bet works. The fund recovers in two years. Risk officers object on the principle of the bet, not the result.', e'Was the bet justified?', 'Money · Trust', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A retirement fund recovered is a retirement fund recovered.', 'A pension is the wrong place to be brave.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A celebrated nonprofit pays its founder more than 60% of its annual donations as salary. Donors find out via a freedom-of-information request. The board says the founder is irreplaceable.', e'Was the salary justified?', 'Money · Trust', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A nonprofit needs the same talent as a company; pay matters.', 'A nonprofit funded by donors is funded for the mission, not the founder.', 'money_wealth', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A man wins a large lottery prize and quietly gives most of it away — to family, to old friends, to causes he believes in — without telling anyone where the money came from. Some recipients later resent the secrecy.', e'Was the secrecy right?', 'Money · Giving', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A gift quietly given is a gift not turned into a transaction.', 'Recipients should know what they are accepting and from whom.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A young couple is gifted a house by his parents on the condition the wife signs a legal document acknowledging the gift was to him alone. She signs. Years later, in a divorce, the house is his.', e'Was the condition fair?', 'Money · Family', 'Fair', 'Unfair', 'A gift can come with the conditions its giver chooses.', 'A gift through one spouse with a legal trap is not a gift to the marriage.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A small-town businessman raises prices sharply during a regional shortage, citing his own higher costs. His competitors do not. He keeps his store open through the shortage; the others run out and close.', e'Was the price hike justified?', 'Money · Crisis', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A store that stays open is a store that served someone.', 'Profiteering on a shortage is profiteering, by any other name.', 'money_wealth', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A debt-collection agency offers a struggling borrower a reduced settlement that requires her to publicly thank them in a testimonial. She refuses and continues to pay the original balance.', e'Was she right to refuse?', 'Money · Dignity', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A settlement that requires public gratitude is humiliation in legal form.', 'A discount is a discount; the rest is words.', 'money_wealth', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A wealthy donor pulls his funding from a university after a student publication criticizes a foundation he chairs. The university says nothing publicly. A dean writes a confidential apology to the donor.', e'Was the dean wrong?', 'Money · Speech', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'A university funded by donors apologizing for student speech is no longer a university.', 'A dean is allowed to defuse a donor without changing a single editorial.', 'money_wealth', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- environment (17)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A small town protests a planned highway through a wetland. The highway would cut commute times for fifty thousand people. The wetland is one of the last in the region. The state proceeds.', e'Was the state right?', 'Env · Trade-offs', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Infrastructure for fifty thousand is the kind of progress a region needs.', 'A wetland that took fifty thousand years cannot be re-grown.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A village allows a foreign mining company to operate in exchange for guaranteed jobs and a school. Five years later the river is contaminated. The school still stands.', e'Was the original deal worth it?', 'Env · Trade', 'Worth it', 'Not worth it', 'A village without jobs has no future to protect.', 'A river poisoned outlasts any school.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A national park introduces a ban on local communities’ traditional gathering practices to preserve the ecosystem. The communities have used the park for generations. Some take their case to court.', e'Was the ban justified?', 'Env · People', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A park collapsing under traditional use cannot continue with it.', 'A people erased from their land is its own kind of ecological loss.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A coastal city restricts new construction within 500 meters of the shore after years of erosion. Property owners lose value overnight. Some sue. The city refuses to compensate.', e'Was the city right not to compensate?', 'Env · Coast', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Property near a vanishing shore was never as valuable as the deed suggested.', 'A government that takes value should pay for it.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A factory promises to switch to clean energy within five years but continues polluting at full capacity in the meantime. Local activists demand an immediate shutdown. The factory employs three thousand people.', e'Should the activists hold the line?', 'Env · Patience', 'Hold the line', 'Concede', 'A five-year promise is a five-year free pass.', 'Three thousand families and a slow shift is the only realistic kind.', 'environment', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A city introduces high parking fees in its core to push commuters toward public transit. Small businesses see footfall drop. The city refuses to roll the policy back.', e'Was the policy justified?', 'Env · Transit', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A city you can park in is a city you cannot breathe in.', 'A policy that empties small businesses is a policy that emptied a neighborhood.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A farmer quietly burns crop residue in violation of state rules because alternatives are too costly. He is fined. He cannot pay. Other farmers in his village do the same.', e'Was the fine justified?', 'Env · Cost', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A rule unenforced is a rule that has stopped existing.', 'A fine without alternatives is a fine on poverty.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A regional government taxes large diesel vehicles by weight, charging the wealthiest owners the most. Civic groups call it fair; some owners call it punitive.', e'Was the tax justified?', 'Env · Fairness', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A polluter-pays system that lands harder on bigger polluters is the design.', 'A tax pegged to wealth is a tax of envy.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A research team flies tens of thousands of miles each year studying climate change. A junior researcher calculates the team’s footprint and proposes a year of remote work. The lead refuses.', e'Was the lead right?', 'Env · Travel', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'In-person field work is non-replaceable for the data the team gathers.', 'A climate team that cannot model its own emissions has missed its own message.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A city installs flood barriers protecting wealthy neighborhoods first, citing higher property value. Poorer areas flood again the next year. The city promises to extend the barriers.', e'Was the order justified?', 'Env · Priorities', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'Higher-value zones generate the tax that funds everything else.', 'A flood barrier built for the rich is a flood that lands on the poor.', 'environment', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A national park raises entry fees fivefold to limit visitor numbers. Lower-income visitors are effectively shut out. The park reports record ecological recovery within two years.', e'Was the fee hike justified?', 'Env · Access', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A park trampled by crowds protects no one.', 'A park accessible only to the rich is no longer a public park.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A city plants thousands of trees in wealthy zones because the soil and budgets are easier. Poorer zones get a few. Five years later, summer mortality in the un-greened zones is up.', e'Was the city wrong?', 'Env · Justice', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'Trees are a public good and have to be planted where they are needed most.', 'A city has to start where it can; gridlocked perfection plants nothing.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A coal-dependent region refuses a federal transition package that offers retraining but requires the closure of two plants within five years. Local leaders say the timeline is too fast.', e'Was the refusal justified?', 'Env · Transition', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A region that loses its industry overnight loses its families.', 'A region that refuses transition is choosing to be left behind.', 'environment', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'An activist publicly destroys a major artwork to draw attention to climate inaction. The artwork is privately owned and was on loan to a public museum. The activist is jailed.', e'Was the act justified?', 'Env · Protest', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A protest that earns attention is a protest doing its job.', 'A climate movement that destroys art is a movement that has lost its case.', 'environment', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A small country sues a wealthier neighbor in international court for emissions that have raised its sea level. The court has limited enforcement powers. The case takes a decade.', e'Was the lawsuit worth filing?', 'Env · Justice', 'Worth filing', 'Not worth filing', 'A lawsuit that creates a record creates accountability for the next decade.', 'A decade in court is a decade not spent rebuilding the coast.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A municipality bans single-use plastics overnight, with no transition support for street vendors. Many vendors are unable to comply and are fined. A few quietly continue.', e'Was the ban justified?', 'Env · Policy', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A clean street is the visible benefit of an unpopular rule.', 'A rule that fines the poor first is a rule designed by the comfortable.', 'environment', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A community successfully blocks a small clean-energy project on aesthetic grounds, citing visual impact. The grid has to procure a more expensive equivalent from a coal plant for the same year.', e'Was the community right?', 'Env · Local', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A community gets to shape what it lives next to.', 'A neighborhood vetoing clean power on aesthetics is a neighborhood deciding for the planet.', 'environment', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- identity_belief (16)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A woman stops attending the family’s religious gatherings after a personal change in belief. Her parents see it as a phase. Her siblings see it as a betrayal. She continues to attend secular family events.', e'Was she right to step back?', 'Belief · Family', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A person’s faith is the one inheritance she chooses.', 'A tradition is a thread; you do not get to cut yours alone.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A man comes out to his elderly grandfather and is told never to come home. Two years later, the grandfather is dying and asks to see him. He refuses to visit.', e'Was he right to refuse?', 'Identity · Forgiveness', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A deathbed is not a forgiveness factory.', 'Two years of rejection deserve one last chance.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A teacher introduces a unit on a contested historical period in a school where most parents share one view. Parents complain. The school removes her from the rotation. She publishes her materials online.', e'Was she right to publish?', 'Belief · History', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A teacher silenced should not stay silent.', 'A classroom’s rules apply outside it; she was paid to follow the curriculum.', 'identity_belief', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A man changes his last name to his wife’s after their wedding. His mother refuses to call him by it. They do not speak for a year. He never reverts.', e'Was the mother right to resist?', 'Identity · Name', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A name carried for generations is not a personal choice alone.', 'A name is the one identity an adult gets to pick.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A young woman wears a particular religious symbol to work despite her employer’s dress code. She is fired. Civil-rights groups defend her. The employer cites a uniform policy.', e'Was the firing justified?', 'Belief · Work', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A workplace gets to set its dress code.', 'A workplace that punishes faith is a workplace that punishes faith.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A father raises his children in a different religion than his own to honor the wishes of his late wife. He never tells the children about his own beliefs. They learn after his death.', e'Was the silence right?', 'Belief · Family', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A promise to a dying wife is a promise.', 'Children deserve to know all the threads they are made of.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A community center bars a religious group from holding meetings on its premises after several members made political statements. The group sues, arguing the bar punishes belief.', e'Was the bar justified?', 'Belief · Space', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A public space gets to set its terms of use.', 'A bar on a group for the speech of a few becomes a bar on belief.', 'identity_belief', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A school principal removes a religious symbol from a classroom in a region where most families share that religion. A few parents thank her quietly. Many demand her resignation.', e'Was the principal right?', 'Belief · School', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A school is a school for every family in it.', 'A symbol the majority lives with is the majority’s right to display.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A man marries into a family of strong religious belief and quietly adopts their practices to keep peace. After his wife’s death, he reverts. The in-laws never speak to him again.', e'Was he right to revert?', 'Belief · Marriage', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A practice held for a partner is held while the partner is here.', 'A man who reverts after a loss reveals what was rented all along.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A young man refuses to attend an arranged ceremony in his family’s tradition that he believes is performative. The family considers it a violation. He calls it a private choice.', e'Was the refusal justified?', 'Belief · Family', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A ritual is consensual or it is theater.', 'A family is a fabric; you cannot pull at a single thread.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A community decides to allow non-members to attend its annual festival for the first time. Some elders object, citing tradition. The festival is open this year. The mood is different.', e'Was the opening right?', 'Belief · Tradition', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A tradition that does not adapt is a tradition that fades alone.', 'A festival opened to all is a festival no longer of one community.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A daughter writes a public essay critical of her mother’s religion. The essay is widely read. Her mother stops speaking to her. The daughter says she never intended the essay to be personal.', e'Was she right to publish?', 'Belief · Voice', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A writer with a story she has lived has a right to write it.', 'A daughter who exposes her mother for an audience has chosen the audience.', 'identity_belief', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A clergy member quietly tells members of his congregation that he no longer believes parts of the doctrine he is paid to teach. He continues to lead services. A few members protest his honesty.', e'Was he right to stay?', 'Belief · Vocation', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A leader who is honest with his community is honest twice over.', 'A man who teaches what he does not believe is a man who is not teaching.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A woman is excluded from a family wedding because she has chosen not to wear traditional dress. She attends the small civil reception only. Older relatives say she made her choice.', e'Was the exclusion justified?', 'Belief · Custom', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A family’s wedding has the family’s rules.', 'A dress code is not the price of admission to your own family.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A clinic refuses to prescribe a medication that violates the doctor’s personal beliefs, even though the patient meets every medical indication. A colleague prescribes within minutes.', e'Was the original doctor justified?', 'Belief · Care', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A doctor’s conscience does not stop existing at the clinic door.', 'A patient denied care over a private belief has been denied a public service.', 'identity_belief', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A young woman publicly leaves a high-profile spiritual community, naming names and practices. Lawyers from the community sue her for defamation. Several members reach out to her privately to thank her.', e'Was she right to publish names?', 'Belief · Speech', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A community that operates by fear can only be checked by names.', 'A public list of names is a sentence handed out without trial.', 'identity_belief', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

