-- crime (16)
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
(e'A neighborhood collectively decides not to report a known thief to police after he begins returning stolen items and apologizing. Months later, he relapses on a different street.', e'Was the neighborhood wrong?', 'Crime · Restoration', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'A relapse on the next street is the price of mercy on this one.', 'A man returning items is a man worth a second chance.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A teenager records a clip of a serious crime and posts it online before calling the police. The clip helps identify the perpetrator. The victim’s family asks the platform to take it down.', e'Was the teenager right?', 'Crime · Evidence', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A viral clip is sometimes the only thing that catches a quiet criminal.', 'A victim does not consent to being broadcast.', 'crime', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A man chases down a pickpocket who took his wallet and beats him until he confesses. The wallet is returned. He is arrested for assault.', e'Was the arrest justified?', 'Crime · Vigilantism', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A society of citizens collecting their own justice is no longer a society.', 'A man who took your money does not get to walk because he was caught.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A bystander records a violent assault but does not intervene. The video helps convict the attacker. The victim sues the bystander for failing to act.', e'Was the lawsuit justified?', 'Crime · Bystanders', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A society is what we do, not what we record.', 'A bystander is not a hero who failed; he is a witness who survived.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A small business owner installs facial-recognition cameras after a string of shoplifting incidents. Customers protest. Shoplifting drops by 80%.', e'Was the install justified?', 'Crime · Surveillance', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A business that does not survive will not serve any customer.', 'A shop that scans every face has stopped being a shop.', 'crime', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A man hires private investigators after the police close his daughter’s missing-person case. He finds new evidence and forces the case to reopen. The investigators broke a few minor laws to get the evidence.', e'Was the man justified?', 'Crime · Justice', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A father is allowed to spend any resource on his daughter’s life.', 'Evidence collected illegally taints every other piece of the case.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A landlord refuses to rent to a man who served time for a non-violent offense ten years ago. The man cannot find housing. The landlord cites his investment risk.', e'Was the refusal justified?', 'Crime · Re-entry', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A landlord is allowed to manage his own risk.', 'A society that bars its returned citizens is the same society that arrested them.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge sentences a parent of three small children to two years in jail for a non-violent offense, citing the law. A social worker argues the family will collapse. The sentence stands.', e'Was the sentence right?', 'Crime · Family', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A law is not weaker because of the family of its offender.', 'A sentence that destroys three children is a sentence with hidden costs.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A whistleblower exposes police corruption and is charged with leaking confidential documents. He is found guilty and serves time. The corruption he exposed leads to dozens of dismissals.', e'Was the conviction justified?', 'Crime · Truth', 'Justified', 'Not justified', 'A leak is a leak; the law cannot exempt people who shake the system.', 'A society that punishes its truth-tellers is a society that stops hearing the truth.', 'crime', 'topical', 'curated'),
(e'A man refuses to testify against a friend whom he saw commit a crime. He is held in contempt and jailed for two months. The case collapses without his testimony.', e'Was he right to refuse?', 'Crime · Loyalty', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'There are loyalties older than the courts.', 'A friend who commits a crime has already broken the friendship.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A small-town pharmacist quietly identifies a serial buyer of suspicious medication and reports him to the police. The buyer is arrested. The pharmacist later learns the buyer was being prescribed off-label by a doctor.', e'Was the pharmacist right?', 'Crime · Caution', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A vigilant citizen is a public safety asset.', 'A pharmacist is not a police officer; he should not act like one.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A defense lawyer argues for the release of a client who confessed to her in private. The confession is privileged. The lawyer secures the release. The client commits a similar crime within a year.', e'Was the lawyer right to defend?', 'Crime · Privilege', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'Without privilege, no defense lawyer hears the truth from any client.', 'A privilege that releases the dangerous is a privilege with blood on it.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A neighborhood watch group reports a man for "looking suspicious" while taking out the trash. The police arrive aggressively. The man is unhurt but humiliated. The watch group declines to apologize.', e'Was the report defensible?', 'Crime · Bias', 'Defensible', 'Indefensible', 'A neighborhood watch is alerted to outliers; that is the system.', 'A watch group that reports neighbors by appearance is the harm it claims to prevent.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A teen vandalizes a war memorial during a protest. He is arrested. The memorial is repaired within a week. The teen is sentenced to two years.', e'Was the sentence proportionate?', 'Crime · Vandalism', 'Proportionate', 'Excessive', 'A society protects its memorials by punishing those who break them.', 'A two-year sentence for a teenager is a wound nobody can repair.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A man finds his neighbor’s door unlocked and a stranger inside. He locks the stranger in until police arrive. The stranger turns out to be the neighbor’s estranged brother, expected later that day.', e'Was the man right?', 'Crime · Caution', 'Right', 'Wrong', 'A neighbor’s instinct is the cheapest security a street has.', 'A man held until police arrive is a man assumed guilty.', 'crime', 'evergreen', 'curated'),
(e'A judge sentences a man to community service instead of jail for repeated minor offenses. The man does the service well. Two years later, he is convicted of a serious crime.', e'Was the original sentence wrong?', 'Crime · Mercy', 'Wrong', 'Not wrong', 'Lenient sentences for small crimes encourage larger ones.', 'A long-term outcome years later is not the verdict on the original decision.', 'crime', 'seasonal', 'curated'),

